Introduction
Most Expert Advisors become distorted versions of their original strategies. We start with clear rules, then add filters after a loss, narrow the time window after a weak session, and add confirmation candles for perceived safety. By the time the code compiles, it is a pile of patches wrapped around a memory of an idea. We drift not because we are careless, but because we never knew how our own strategy behaved. We knew the rules; we did not know the mechanics—whether wide ranges break better than narrow ones, whether breakout volume matters, whether a retest is a healthy sign or a warning. Without that knowledge, every development decision is a guess.
Instead of coding the Expert Advisor first, build an instrument that observes the market and records what the strategy would have seen. It does not trade or make decisions. It follows our initial rules, captures every component of the strategy as a measurable number, and writes the result to a file. We then hand that file to the MT5 AI Assistant and work through structured questions about what actually happened: what separates a genuine breakout from a false one, what the winners have in common, and when we should be doing nothing at all. By the time we open MetaEditor to write the real Expert Advisor, we are encoding evidence rather than guessing.
System Overview
The strategy we are instrumenting is the Range Breakout, chosen because it is simple and widely used. In Session Breakout mode the user picks Sydney, Tokyo, London, or New York. In Time-based Breakout mode the user supplies a start and end time in HH:MM form. Both modes feed the same measurement engine, so evidence collected from different windows stays comparable. The logger's life cycle has three stages, and every column in the output belongs to one of them. The range stage accumulates the high, low, tick volume, and bar count while the window is open. When the window closes, the range is frozen and stamped with ATR, the previous day's high and low, and the prior session's boundaries. The breakout stage captures the first bar whose close exits the range, along with its size, body, penetration distance, and relative volume. The tracking stage follows the next twenty-four bars and measures retest behavior, favorable and adverse excursion, and the final outcome.
The key design choice is separating stages. Range and breakout fields are available when the breakout candle closes and can be used for entry. Tracking fields are only known afterward. Maximum favorable excursion, retest depth, and the outcome label itself can only be known afterwards. If we mix the two, we build an Expert Advisor that looks profitable in analysis and collapses in live trading, because it is quietly using information from the future. The logger writes both halves into the same file but labels them clearly, and the first thing we ask the AI to do is enforce that boundary.
Outcome classification uses a first-touch rule. An event is SUCCESS if favorable excursion reaches one full range width before price closes more than halfway back inside the range, FALSE if the reverse happens first, and NEUTRAL if neither occurs within the tracking window. When both trigger on the same bar, the row carries an ambiguity flag so those events can be excluded rather than guessed at. This is a deliberately strict definition, and it produces a sobering baseline.
