RangeInfo holds everything known about a range once its window closes, with the valid flag gating out fragments that would poison the statistics. The single most important line in the second structure is RangeInfo rng. Each event carries its own frozen copy of the parent range rather than a reference to the live global. Without it, a second breakout or the arrival of the next session would overwrite range data underneath an event still being tracked. By copying at the moment the breakout fires, every row describes the world exactly as it was when the decision would have been made. This is the structural guarantee against contaminated data, and it is far easier to get right in a logger than to debug inside a live Expert Advisor.
Resolving the time window
bool
InWindow(
const
int
m)
  {
if
(g_winStartMin == g_winEndMin)
return
false
;
if
(g_winStartMin < g_winEndMin)
return
(m >= g_winStartMin && m < g_winEndMin);
return
(m >= g_winStartMin || m < g_winEndMin);
  }
Both range modes are reduced to a pair of minute-of-day values, and membership is tested against those rather than against dates. The third return statement handles the wrap-around case where the window opens before midnight and closes after it. The Sydney session runs 23:00 to 06:00 and would break any implementation assuming start is always less than end. Because of this, session mode and custom time mode share one code path.
