The entry price deserves attention. When the trigger is a close, we record the actual close of the breakout candle, because that is where a bar-close Expert Advisor would enter. When the trigger is a wick, we record the buffered trigger level—the boundary offset by the ATR buffer—because that is where a resting stop order would actually have filled. Using the close in both cases would overstate the entry quality of wick-triggered breakouts and quietly corrupt every excursion measurement that follows. The volume ratio is computed against the average of the bars immediately preceding the breakout rather than against the range average, which makes it a measure of surge at the moment of the break rather than a measure of how busy the session was overall. Both concepts turn out to be useful, and the file carries them as separate columns.
Tracking an event
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Track Event                                                      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
TrackEvent(BreakEvent &e,
const
MqlRates
&b)
  {
   e.barsTracked++;
int
k = e.barsTracked;
double
fav = (e.dir >
0
) ? (b.high - e.brkPrice) : (e.brkPrice - b.low);
double
adv = (e.dir >
0
) ? (e.brkPrice - b.low)  : (b.high - e.brkPrice);
if
(fav > e.mfe)
     {
      e.mfe    = fav;
      e.mfeBar = k;
     }
if
(adv > e.mae)
     {
      e.mae    = adv;
      e.maeBar = k;
     }
if
(!e.followNset && k == InpFollowBars)
     {
      e.followN    = (e.dir >
0
) ? (b.close - e.brkPrice) : (e.brkPrice - b.close);
      e.followNset =
true
;
     }
//--- retest bookkeeping
double
tol = InpRetestTolATR * e.rng.atr;
if
(!e.retestSeen)
     {
bool
touched = (e.dir >
0
) ? (b.low <= e.edge + tol) : (b.high >= e.edge - tol);
if
(touched)
        {
         e.retestSeen    =
true
;
         e.retestBar     = k;
         e.retestExtreme = (e.dir >
0
) ? b.low : b.high;
        }
     }
else
if
(e.retestDurBars <
0
)
        {
if
(e.dir >
0
&& b.low  < e.retestExtreme)
            e.retestExtreme = b.low;
if
(e.dir <
0
&& b.high > e.retestExtreme)
            e.retestExtreme = b.high;
bool
resumed = (e.dir >
0
) ? (b.close > e.brkHigh) : (b.close < e.brkLow);
if
(resumed)
           {
            e.retestDurBars = k - e.retestBar;
            e.retestHeld    =
true
;
           }
        }
//--- did the bar close back inside the range at all
bool
inside = (e.dir >
0
) ? (b.close < e.rng.high) : (b.close > e.rng.low);
if
(inside)
      e.closedInside =
true
;
//--- outcome resolution, first touch wins
if
(e.outcome ==
0
)
     {
bool
hitTarget = (e.mfe >= InpSuccessR * e.rUnit);
double
failLvl = (e.dir >
0
)
                       ? e.rng.high - (InpFailInsidePct /
100.0
) * e.rng.size
                       : e.rng.low  + (InpFailInsidePct /
100.0
) * e.rng.size;
bool
hitFail = (e.dir >
0
) ? (b.close < failLvl) : (b.close > failLvl);
if
(hitTarget && hitFail)
         e.ambiguous =
true
;
//--- same-bar collision: bar data cannot tell us which came first,
//--- so leave the event undecided rather than guessing SUCCESS
else
if
(hitTarget)
        {
         e.outcome      =
1
;
         e.barsToTarget = k;
        }
else
if
(hitFail)
           {
            e.outcome    = -
1
;
            e.barsToFail = k;
           }
     }
  }
Three things happen on every tracked bar. Excursions are measured from the recorded entry price with the direction sign applied, and the bar index is stored alongside each extreme—that index is what later distinguishes a breakout that peaked immediately and died from one that built steadily.
Retest detection runs as a two-state machine. The first branch waits for price to return within tolerance of the broken boundary; the second tracks how deep it goes and watches for a close back beyond the breakout candle's extreme, which is our definition of the retest holding. Retest depth turns out to be the most decisive behavioral difference between winners and losers in the dataset.
Outcome resolution is guarded by e.outcome == 0, implementing the first-touch rule: once resolved, the verdict is locked, though tracking continues so excursion statistics stay complete. When both conditions trigger on the same bar the ambiguity flag is set, because bar data cannot tell us which came first. Marking that uncertainty costs one boolean column and prevents a whole class of silent error. Because the flag now blocks the outcome assignment as well as marking the row, ambiguous events fall through as undecided and never enter the SUCCESS or FALSE counts.
The per-bar orchestration
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Per-bar processing                                               |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
ProcessBar(
const
MqlRates
&b,
const
int
shift)
  {
int
mod
= MinuteOfDay(b.time);
bool
inWin  = InWindow(
mod
);
//--- window edges
if
(inWin && !g_prevInWin)
     {
      FlushActive(
true
);
// a new window ends any open tracking
StartRange(b);
      g_prevInWin =
true
;
return
;
     }
if
(inWin && g_prevInWin)
     {
      UpdateRange(b);
      g_prevInWin =
true
;
return
;
     }
if
(!inWin && g_prevInWin)
     {
      FreezeRange(shift);
      g_prevInWin =
false
;
//--- this bar already sits outside the window, so let it be examined below
}

   g_prevInWin =
false
;
if
(!g_range.valid)
return
;
//--- track everything already open
for
(
int
i =
0
; i <
ArraySize
(g_events); i++)
     {
if
(!g_events[i].active)
continue
;
      TrackEvent(g_events[i], b);
if
(g_events[i].barsTracked >= InpTrackBars)
         FinalizeEvent(g_events[i],
false
);
     }
//--- hunt for new breakouts
g_range.barsSinceEnd++;
if
(g_range.barsSinceEnd > InpBreakWindowBar)
return
;
if
(g_range.events >= InpMaxEvents)
return
;
double
buffer  = InpBufferATR * g_range.atr;
double
upLevel = g_range.high + buffer;
double
dnLevel = g_range.low  - buffer;
double
refUp = (InpTrigger == TRIGGER_CLOSE) ? b.close : b.high;
double
refDn = (InpTrigger == TRIGGER_CLOSE) ? b.close : b.low;
bool
allowUp = !(InpOnePerSide && g_range.brokeUp);
bool
allowDn = !(InpOnePerSide && g_range.brokeDown);
if
(allowUp && refUp > upLevel)
      OpenEvent(
1
, b, shift, upLevel);
if
(g_range.events < InpMaxEvents && allowDn && refDn < dnLevel)
      OpenEvent(-
1
, b, shift, dnLevel);
  }
The orchestration runs on edge detection rather than date arithmetic, which is what makes wrap-around sessions work without special cases. On the rising edge any events still being tracked are flushed with the truncated flag set—an event cut short by the next session did not reach its natural conclusion, and marking it lets the analysis handle those rows rather than treating a partial observation as complete. On the falling edge the range is frozen and the same bar falls through to be examined for a breakout. Both directions are then tested against the buffered boundary, since a narrow range in high volatility can in principle be pierced on both sides by a single bar.
