Conclusion
The reason so many Expert Advisors end up as distorted versions of the strategies that inspired them is that we begin coding before we understand how our own rules behave. In this article we inverted that order. We built a Range Breakout logger that takes no trades, supports session-based and time-based range definitions, and records sixty-one measurements per breakout, then ran it across twenty months of GBPUSD Tokyo sessions and interrogated a systematic 42-event sample of the resulting 419 breakouts in nine structured phases. What emerged contradicted most of what we would have assumed. Volume confirmation showed no separation between winners and losers in this sample. Breakout candle size, range size, ATR level, and time of day were similarly flat. Two features carried almost all the signal: where the range sits relative to prior-day structure on the breakout side, and how clean the breakout candle's body is. The strongest separators in the entire dataset—retest depth and whether the retest held—turned out to be post-entry by construction, useful for managing a trade and useless for selecting one.
The analysis also produced warnings we would not have received any other way. Four field definitions could not be verified from the data alone. One row carries a corrupted spread. Roughly fourteen weekdays are missing from the file. And every statistic from the baseline onward rests on a ten percent sample rather than the full dataset, which the assistant stated plainly instead of presenting sampled figures as exhaustive ones. The honest position at the end of Part 1 is therefore that we have a strong candidate signal, a clear ranking of what does not work, and a specific list of things to verify before any of it becomes code. Nothing here has been checked against the 90% of the data the sample left out, so every threshold is a candidate to validate on that held-out set—or on a later period—before it earns any confidence.
What transfers is the method rather than the numbers. The logger here is specific to Range Breakout, but the structure suits any strategy: define the components, instrument them, record the outcome, separate what is knowable at entry from what is only knowable in hindsight, and interrogate the evidence in phases before writing a line of trading logic. In Part 2 we build the Expert Advisor from exactly this evidence. Structure location becomes the primary entry condition with its threshold justified by the stability of the zone rather than the peak of the win rate, body ratio becomes the secondary filter, the retest findings shape exit logic with the constraint that they must be replicated live rather than read from a file, and the UP/DOWN asymmetry is carried as a risk adjustment rather than a separate rule set. The question Part 2 answers is whether evidence-led development actually produces a better Expert Advisor than the intuition-led approach it was designed to replace.
